   </measurement>
   <debugger title="6. Structured captured inspection">
     <p><DEBUGGER-PROTOCOL fact="true" status="spec/proposed">The shared debugger route registers strict epoch-bound inspect, tree, explain, cost, compare, capture and inspection-only cancel requests and result/error/progress carriers. CLI structured input and MCP delegate to one host-bound route retaining immutable M16 captures, original-byte position basis and snapshot/result identities. Page sessions retain exact context, capture and filter; stale, replayed or foreign cursors refuse. Stream replay uses the incumbent event model and reports gaps explicitly. Cancelling inspection never requests or claims build cancellation. Captured costs retain the incumbent measurement recipe, unavailable tokenizer/billing/ambient-tail facts and no boot admission quota. Reads never invoke compilation, providers, retention, installation or model activation. Host-granted capture and overlay authority remain separate from transport values. Finite captures are not atomic all-files observations, source authentication or hostile-writer proofs.</DEBUGGER-PROTOCOL></p>
+    <p><DEBUGGER-CAPTURED-JOINS fact="true" status="spec/proposed">Snapshot comparison uses active captured resource presence, not the declaration inventory. A successful finite capture proves bytes or absence; unavailable reads refuse capture rather than becoming absence. Creation and deletion are added and removed; changed requires bytes on both sides. Graph source indices resolve through their retained identity catalogue and are not host resource indices. Available emitted-artifact spans retain one-based inclusive line meaning and exact snapshot, artifact resource and digest. Absent spans and unproved authored-resource joins remain explicit null values; emitted lines never become authored XML coordinates. The registered compiler carrier remains available with its original format identity when captured, preserving source trees and positions without reconstruction.</DEBUGGER-CAPTURED-JOINS></p>
   </debugger>
 </spec>
